    "keyNotes": "• Nature of Worship in African Traditional Religion (ATR):\n  - Worship is a communal and holistic way of life rather than a one-hour weekly routine.\n  - The African spiritual hierarchy:\n    1. The Supreme Being (God / Onyankopon / Mawu / Odomankoma).\n    2. The Lesser Deities (Abosom / Vodowo / Trowo) who act as intermediaries or ministers of God.\n    3. The Ancestors (Nsamanfo / Togbewo) who are departed righteous elders watching over their living descendants.\n    4. Sacred inanimate objects and charms (fetishes / talismans / amulets).\n• Libation (Apesato / Nsa gu):\n  - The ritual pouring of liquids (water, palm wine, schnapps, gin) accompanied by prayers.\n  - Three Systematic Stages of Pouring Libation:\n    1. Invocation: The officiant calls upon the Supreme Being first, followed by Mother Earth (Asaase Yaa), the lesser deities, and finally the ancestors by name.\n    2. Supplication (Petitions): Pleading for blessings, long life, rain, bountiful harvest, peace, fertility, good health, and success for the community or individual.\n    3. Conclusion & Curses/Imprecation: Wishing well for benefactors and asking judgment/curses upon stubborn enemies, witches, and evil saboteurs.\n• Sacrifices and Offerings:\n  - Blood sacrifices (chickens, sheep, cows) and non-blood offerings (yams, eggs, mashed yam/eto, palm oil).\n  - Types of Sacrifices:\n    * Thanksgiving sacrifices (expressing joy for a newborn, victory, or bumper harvest).\n    * Votive / Vow sacrifices (fulfilling a pledge made after receiving divine help).\n    * Expiation / Cleansing sacrifices (atoning for a broken taboo, adultery, or community defilement).\n    * Preventive sacrifices (warding off predicted calamities, epidemics, or premature deaths).\n• Moral and Communal Significance of ATR Worship:\n  - Reinforces continuous accountability to the ancestors who punish secret wickedness and reward virtuous conduct.\n  - Fosters social cohesion, reverence for elders, and community solidarity.",
