    "youtubeUrl": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0R1H3X7o1kM",
    "youtubeId": "0R1H3X7o1kM",
    "keyNotes": "• The Two Canonical Festivals in Islam (The Two Eids):\n  1. Eid-ul-Fitr (Festival of Breaking the Fast):\n     - Celebrated on the 1st of Shawwal (the 10th Islamic lunar month) following the mandatory month of Ramadan.\n     - Significance of Ramadan: Month in which the Holy Quran was revealed (Laylat-al-Qadr / Night of Power). Muslims fast (Sawm) from dawn to dusk, abstaining from food, drink, and sexual relations to cultivate Taqwa (God-consciousness).\n     - Core Ritual: Zakat-ul-Fitr (mandatory food charity given to the poor before the Eid prayer, ensuring no one goes hungry).\n     - Activities: Congregational outdoor Eid prayer, listening to the Khutbah, wearing new or best clean clothes, greetings ('Eid Mubarak!'), visiting relatives, and sharing sweet dishes.\n     - Moral lessons: Self-discipline, empathy for the poor, generosity, gratitude to Allah, and spiritual renewal.\n  2. Eid-ul-Adha (The Feast of Sacrifice / Eid-el-Kabir):\n     - Celebrated on the 10th of Dhul-Hijjah (the 12th Islamic month), coinciding with the climax of the annual Hajj pilgrimage in Makkah.\n     - Historical Background: Commemorates the supreme obedience of Prophet Ibrahim (Abraham) when tested by Allah to sacrifice his beloved son Ismail (Ishmael). Allah ransomed Ismail with a ram when Ibrahim proved his total willingness to obey.\n     - Core Ritual: Slaughtering an animal (ram, goat, sheep, cow, camel) in the name of Allah.\n     - Sharing the Meat: The meat is divided into three equal shares:\n       * One-third for the family.\n       * One-third for friends, neighbors, and relatives.\n       * One-third donated to the destitute and poor.\n     - Moral lessons: Total obedience to God, supreme sacrifice, charity, hospitality, and communal brotherhood.\n• Differences Between Eid-ul-Fitr and Eid-ul-Adha:\n  - Eid-ul-Fitr marks the end of 29/30 days of Ramadan fasting; Eid-ul-Adha commemorates Ibrahim's sacrifice during Hajj.\n  - Zakat-ul-Fitr (food grains) is unique to Eid-ul-Fitr; animal slaughtering (Udhiyyah/Qurbani) is unique to Eid-ul-Adha.",
