    "youtubeUrl": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Uq3zK2_z2t0",
    "youtubeId": "Uq3zK2_z2t0",
    "keyNotes": "• Birth and Upbringing:\n  - Born in Makkah in 570 CE ('Year of the Elephant') into the noble Hashim clan of the Quraysh tribe.\n  - His father Abdullah died before his birth; his mother Aminah died when he was six years old.\n  - Fostered in the desert by Halimah as an infant; raised by his grandfather Abdul Muttalib, and after his death, by his protective uncle Abu Talib.\n  - Worked as a shepherd and later managed trading caravans across Arabia and Syria.\n• Reputation for Exceptional Integrity:\n  - The people of Makkah nicknamed him 'Al-Amin' (The Trustworthy) and 'As-Sadiq' (The Truthful) because he never lied, cheated, or worshipped idols.\n  - Impressed by his honesty, a wealthy businesswoman named Khadijah employed him and later proposed marriage to him.\n• The Call to Prophethood (610 CE):\n  - At age forty, while meditating in solitude at Cave Hira on Mount Nur, Angel Jibril (Gabriel) appeared to him with the first revelation of the Holy Quran:\n    'Iqra! (Read / Proclaim in the name of your Lord who created...)' - Surah Al-Alaq 96:1-5.\n  - Khadijah was the first person to believe in his prophethood, followed by his cousin Ali, his servant Zayd, and his friend Abu Bakr.\n• Persecution in Makkah and Early Trials:\n  - The Quraysh leaders severely persecuted early Muslims (e.g. torturing the slave Bilal ibn Rabah) because Islam preached Tawhid (Oneness of God) and condemned idolatry, infanticide of infant girls, and social oppression.\n• The Hijrah to Madinah (622 CE):\n  - Due to assassination plots by the Quraysh, Muhammad and Abu Bakr migrated 320 km north to Yathrib (renamed Madinat un-Nabi - The City of the Prophet).\n  - The Hijrah marks Year 1 of the Islamic lunar calendar (1 AH).\n• Moral and Leadership Virtues of the Prophet:\n  - Unshakable patience (Sabr) in adversity, deep honesty in commerce, championing women's rights and freeing slaves, and forgiving his bitter enemies upon the conquest of Makkah.",
