    "keyNotes": "• The Importance of Good Manners in Ghanaian Society:\n  - Manners are socially acceptable behaviors, etiquette, and polite conduct that express respect, honor, and decency in interpersonal relationships.\n  - An Akan proverb states: 'Obi mfa ne nsa benkum nkyere n'agya akura' (No one uses the left hand to show the direction to their father's hometown).\n• Core Courtesies and Cultural Etiquette in Ghana:\n  1. Greetings:\n     - Always greet elders first when entering a room or meeting people on the road.\n     - In traditional gatherings, greeting progresses from RIGHT to LEFT.\n     - Never use the LEFT hand to greet, point, wave, or hand items to an elder (the left hand is culturally perceived as unclean).\n     - Men remove hats when greeting chiefs or entering palaces/churches.\n  2. Decency in Speech:\n     - Using polite words like 'Please' (Mepa wo kyew), 'Thank you' (Medase), 'Excuse me' (Kafra), and 'I am sorry'.\n     - Avoiding foul language, insulting elderly people, gossiping, and backbiting.\n  3. Modesty in Dressing:\n     - Dressing neatly and appropriately according to the occasion (school, church, mosque, funeral).\n     - Avoiding overly revealing or tight clothing that violates cultural modesty.\n• Essential Moral Virtues for Youth:\n  - Honesty and Truthfulness: Speaking the truth without deceit even when it is painful or costly.\n  - Humility: Recognizing one's limits, accepting correction, and rejecting boastful arrogance.\n  - Hard Work and Diligence: Rejecting laziness, cheating, and 'get-rich-quick' schemes in favor of disciplined effort.\n  - Patience and Self-Control: Controlling one's temper, sexual impulses, and appetites.\n• Social Vices to Shun:\n  - Drug abuse (tramadol, marijuana, alcohol), cyber fraud (sakawa), teenage pregnancy, examination malpractice, theft, gambling, and occultism.\n• Rewards of Living a Virtuous Life:\n  - Peace of mind, trust from elders, good community reputation, academic excellence, and divine favor.",
